<h2 id="story">E Ink Carta 1400 May Not Arrive Until 2027</h2>
<p>The next big step up in e-book panel technology may not land this year after all. On September 4, 2026, <a href="https://goodereader.com/blog/e-book-news/e-ink-carta-1400-might-be-pushed-back-to-2027" rel="nofollow noopener">Good e-Reader reported</a>, citing supply-chain sources, that <strong>E Ink's Carta 1400 screen technology won't be ready for mass production until later in 2027</strong> &mdash; not the debut later this year that had previously been rumored. If the report holds, the most anticipated black-and-white e-paper panel in years will miss this year's Kindle and Kobo refresh cycles entirely.</p>
<p>The same report says E Ink's three largest manufacturing partners are already holding sample units to evaluate how the new screen fits into future devices: <strong>Foxconn</strong>, which builds the Amazon Kindle; <strong>Netronix</strong>, which manufactures Kobo and NOOK e-readers; and <strong>Linfiny</strong>, which focuses on B2B e-note and display products. Sample access to all three partners that early is a strong signal the technology is real &mdash; the question is timing, not existence. Shipping sample panels to your biggest customers and then telling them, months later, that mass production has slipped is precisely how a display-glass roadmap delay shows up in the real world.</p>
<p>Because this is a supply-chain sourcing report rather than an official E Ink announcement, treat the late-2027 date as directional rather than final. Supply-chain timelines for display panels slip often, and they occasionally jump forward too. What makes this particular rumor worth watching is not just the date but the cascade effect it has on the <em>entire</em> e-reader roadmap you care about as an EPUB reader.</p>

<h2 id="what-carta-1400-promises">What Carta 1400 Was Supposed to Bring</h2>
<p>E Ink panels are named and released in generations &mdash; Carta 1200, then 1300, then 1400 &mdash; and each new generation is what e-reader makers bake into next year's Kindle Paperwhite, Kobo Clara, and PocketBook models. If you buy an e-reader in 2026 or 2027, the panel inside it determines how crisp the text is, how snappy page turns feel, and how much ghosting you see between pages. That is why a panel-roadmap delay is more than a supply-chain logistics story for anyone who actually reads EPUBs on a dedicated device.</p>
<p>Per the Good e-Reader report, the rumored Carta 1400 improvements center on three concrete things:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Higher optical contrast ratio past 20:1</strong> for crisper, truer blacks and brighter whites &mdash; the single biggest readability factor for e-ink, especially outdoors and in low light.</li>
<li><strong>Lower latency</strong> so page turns, scrolling, and handwriting feel much closer to an iPad or a paper notebook than today's e-ink.</li>
<li><strong>Refined waveform technology</strong> to minimize text ghosting and the dramatic &ldquo;black flash&rdquo; that still happens on full e-ink screen refreshes.</li>
</ul>
<p>For context, Carta 1300 &mdash; the current mainstream panel found in most modern Kindles, Kobos, and PocketBooks &mdash; was itself an incremental refinement of Carta 1200 rather than a ground-up redesign. Carta 1200 was the architectural overhaul that brought much faster refresh to e-paper; Carta 1300 sharpened the contrast and reduced latency on top of that same foundation. Carta 1400 was billed as the next meaningful jump in that same line, which is exactly why its slip matters.</p>

<h2 id="color-cascade">The Delay Cascades to Color Panels Too</h2>
<p>The most consequential part of the report for the broader e-reader market is the knock-on effect on color. Good e-Reader states that the Carta 1400 delay <strong>cascades to Kaleido 4 and Gallery 4</strong>, E Ink's next color technologies, which are currently in the prototyping stage. Those color panels will not be commercially ready until Carta 1400 first lands in black-and-white e-readers such as the Kindle Paperwhite and other future monochrome devices.</p>
<p>That ordering &mdash; monochrome Carta first, then color built on the same underlying tech &mdash; is how E Ink has structured its roadmap before. Color e-paper has always trailed the black-and-white line because color panels share the same base architecture plus an extra color-filter layer that is harder to make. If Carta 1400 slips to late 2027, any e-reader you buy that relies on the <em>next</em> generation of color (Kaleido 4 or Gallery 4) probably won't show up until 2028 at the earliest. The color e-readers available today, which we have covered on this site, run on the Kaleido 3 or Gallery 3 generation and remain a separate, already-shipping product category.</p>

<h2 id="means-for-your-library">What This Means for Your EPUB Library Right Now</h2>
<p>Here is the practical takeaway for anyone who reads EPUB files on a dedicated reader, and why a panel delay is actually good news for most buyers in 2026.</p>
<h3>1. Today's e-readers will not feel obsolete any time soon</h3>
<p>If you are reading EPUBs on a current Carta 1300 device &mdash; a Kindle Paperwhite, Kobo Clara or Libra, or a PocketBook &mdash; nothing about the Carta 1400 slip changes your experience. Your screen is not getting worse; the <em>next</em> screen is just taking longer to arrive. EPUB is device-independent: the same <code>.epub</code> file you sideload into Calibre and push to any reader today opens identically on a hypothetical Carta 1400 reader in 2028. There is no format risk in buying now.</p>
<h3>2. If you were waiting for Carta 1400, stop waiting</h3>
<p>The most common buyer's question every e-reader refresh cycle is &ldquo;should I wait for the next panel?&rdquo; With Carta 1400 now pointing to late 2027 &mdash; and consumer devices using it following even later &mdash; the answer for 2026 is clearly <strong>no, buy now</strong>. A promised-but-delayed panel does you no good while you're reading EPUBs today. The current generation of Carta 1300 readers is mature, well-priced, and covers the full range from budget to premium.</p>
<h3>3. Interest rate hikes are the swing factor</h3>
<p>There is one wrinkle worth weighing against the &ldquo;buy now&rdquo; advice. As we wrote in our analysis of the <a href="/blog/amazon-memory-crisis-60-percent-device-price-hike-2026/">Amazon device price hikes driven by the memory crisis</a>, e-reader and component prices have been climbing through 2026. E Ink panel makers have also cut their revenue forecasts this year, which you can trace back to the same memory-cost pressure covered in our <a href="/blog/e-ink-memory-costs-ereader-prices-2026/">E Ink memory-costs deep dive</a>. If component prices keep rising, a delayed Carta 1400 doesn't just push features back &mdash; it raises the odds that whatever does ship in 2027 costs more. That strengthens the case for buying a current-gen reader on sale rather than waiting for an uncertain future device.</p>

<h2 id="should-you-wait">The Bottom Line: Buy the Reader That Fits Your EPUB Habits Now</h2>
<p>A panel-roadmap delay is a tool for planning, not panic. E Ink sells display glass; what you actually read on is the device in your hand or the one on your desk. When you factor in the Carta 1400 slip to late 2027, the color knock-on into 2028, and rising component prices, the smart move for most EPUB readers is to choose a current-generation device based on your real reading habits &mdash; screen size, warm light, waterproofing, sideloading ease &mdash; rather than hold out for hardware that keeps receding.</p>
<p>If portability and a phone-sized EPUB reader appeal to you, the recently announced <a href="/blog/pocketbook-q-mini-phone-sized-ereader-2026/">PocketBook Q mini</a> is a current Carta 1300 option worth a closer look. If you want a color screen for comics and illustrated EPUBs, our <a href="/blog/color-e-ink-readers-2026/">color e-ink readers guide</a> breaks down the Kaleido 3 options shipping today. And if you are weighing Kindle against Kobo against PocketBook more broadly, the <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">three-way comparison</a> on this site walks through the sideloading and EPUB-handling differences that matter more than the panel generation inside.</p>
<p>Whichever reader you land on, remember that your EPUB library is portable, DRM-free, and future-proof in a way no single screen generation can lock you out of. Put your money into the reading experience you want <em>now</em>, and treat Carta 1400 &mdash; whenever it actually ships &mdash; as an optional upgrade later. Good e-Reader's report is worth bookmarking as the reference if you want to track whether the late-2027 date holds.</p>
